>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `register_generator_family`

``` hol4
Refute.register_generator_family :
  {tyop : {Thy : string, Tyop : string}, constructors : term list,
   canonical : term_postprocessor option} -> unit
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Registers a Quickcheck generator for every concrete instance of a type
operator, built from constructor constants whose declared result type
is `tyop` applied to distinct type variables (e.g. `FEMPTY`/`FUPDATE`
for `:'a |-> 'b`).

Unlike `Refute.register_generator`, nothing is stored per instance:
`tyop` is matched on demand, so the registration fires for a concrete
type never previously seen, and the generator, cardinality, and
enumeration caches are invalidated for every instance already cached.
The resulting generator always has `exhaustive = false`, so QC never
reports `NoCounterexample` for a goal universally quantified over a
variable at one of these types; only a closed fact free of such a
variable can be decided that way.

`canonical`, if present, is a display-only `term_postprocessor` fed as a
second registration source into the same walk
`Refute.register_term_postprocessor` drives, composing with every
pattern-registered postprocessor rather than running as a second, separate
walk.  It applies to a reported candidate binding at that type, after
testing and certification have already run against the raw term.  It is
expected to remove structure that a later part of the same candidate has
overwritten (e.g. collapsing `FEMPTY |+ (1,2) |+ (1,3)` to
`FEMPTY |+ (1,3)`); it need not identify every pair of terms that denote
the same value, since that is in general undecidable from syntax alone.

### Failure

Raises `Fail` if `constructors` is empty, if any constructor is not a
constant, if any constructor's result type is not `tyop` applied to
distinct type variables, or if `tyop` already has `TypeBase` datatype
constructors of its own (registering a family for such a type would
silently disable finite exhaustion for what may be a genuinely finite
type).

### See also

[`Refute.register_generator`](#Refute.register_generator),
[`Refute.abstract_generator`](#Refute.abstract_generator),
[`Refute.register_term_postprocessor`](#Refute.register_term_postprocessor)

Model reconstruction is described in \ref{Description:the-holrefute-library}.
